1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,740
Okay so, today I want to talk about the

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,040
biggest mistake I see junior developers make in interviews.

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:10,790
Last year I sat in on forty-one interviews at

4
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:13,370
a company with about two hundred people.

5
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:17,120
Thirty-three of those candidates made the exact same mistake

6
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:18,980
in the first five minutes.

7
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:22,370
And it has nothing to do with code.

8
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,220
Here is the mistake.

9
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:28,970
They start solving the problem before they understand it.

10
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,280
A candidate gets a prompt,

11
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,500
something like design a URL shortener,

12
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,800
and within ten seconds they are writing a database

13
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:37,220
schema.

14
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:40,250
The interviewer has not said a word.

15
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:42,920
Um, you know, it feels productive.

16
00:00:43,430 --> 00:00:44,870
It looks like confidence.

17
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,760
It is actually the fastest way to fail.

18
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:53,330
Let me tell you about Priya.

19
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,640
Priya was a strong candidate,

20
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,940
she had two years of experience and a clean

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:00:59,360
portfolio.

22
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:01,670
She got the same prompt.

23
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:04,340
She stopped, looked at the interviewer,

24
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:05,840
and asked four questions.

25
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:08,150
How many links a day?

26
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,100
Does it need analytics?

27
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:11,690
Can links expire?

28
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,640
Who is the user?

29
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:15,590
That took ninety seconds.

30
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,340
The interviewer told me afterwards that was the moment

31
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,200
she decided to recommend her.

32
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:24,950
Priya got the offer, and she negotiated eleven percent

33
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:26,450
above the first number.

34
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:31,100
So here is what I want you to do.

35
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:33,770
Next time you get a prompt,

36
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:36,710
do not touch the keyboard for two minutes.

37
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:40,460
Ask questions until you can say the problem back

38
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:41,600
in one sentence.

39
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:45,350
Then write it down on the screen where the

40
00:01:45,410 --> 00:01:46,850
interviewer can see it.

41
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,520
I call this the restate step,

42
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:52,820
and it works because it turns a test into

43
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:53,600
a conversation.

44
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:58,250
Now, there is a second part that nobody talks

45
00:01:58,310 --> 00:01:58,670
about.

46
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:02,420
After you restate, pick one thing and say it

47
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,720
out loud: here is what I will build first,

48
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,300
and here is what I will skip.

49
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:11,690
Interviewers are not looking for a perfect system.

50
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:15,440
They are looking for someone who knows what to

51
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:16,220
leave out.

52
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:19,970
We hired a candidate who built a tiny version

53
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:23,270
in thirty minutes over a candidate who drew a

54
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:25,130
huge diagram and never finished.

55
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,700
If you only remember one thing,

56
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:29,480
remember this.

57
00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:32,510
The first two minutes decide the interview.

58
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,180
Spend them on questions, not code.

59
00:02:35,690 --> 00:02:38,930
Try it in your next mock interview and tell

60
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:41,510
me how it goes in the comments.

